**Figure 14.5** illustrates the Embedding layer as a **dictionary mapping ints to floating-point vectors**. Four tokens ("who", "rabbit", "arrow", "lodge") pass through a **Tokenizer**, which produces integers 0-7. The highlighted token "arrow" becomes **3**, and integer 3 selects **row 3** of the embedding table (an 8 × 8 grid of gray-level float values). That row is the vector for "arrow".

When an `Embedding` layer is created, its weights, its internal dictionary of token vectors, are **initially random**, as with any layer. During training, backpropagation gradually adjusts these vectors, structuring the space into something the downstream model can exploit. Once fully trained, the space shows a lot of structure, **specialized for the specific problem** being trained on.

**Listing 14.24** builds the benchmark model, `lstm_with_embedding`:

- `hidden_dim = 64`; integer inputs of shape `(max_length,)`;
- `keras.layers.Embedding(input_dim=max_tokens, output_dim=hidden_dim, mask_zero=True)`;
- then exactly as in Listing 14.21: `Bidirectional(LSTM(hidden_dim))`, `Dropout(0.5)`, `Dense(1, activation="sigmoid")`;
- compiled with `adam`, `binary_crossentropy` and `accuracy`.

The only change from the one-hot model is the input representation: a learned 64-dimensional vector per token instead of a fixed 30,000-dimensional one-hot vector.

The authors then explain the first two arguments. **`input_dim`** sets the range of possible integer inputs, that is, how many keys the lookup dictionary has. **`output_dim`** sets the dimensionality of the vectors looked up, that is, the dimensionality of the structured word space. The third argument, `mask_zero=True`, is explained on the next page (p.436).
